// Read is permitted iff Heartbeat has held continuously since a
// Login — i.e. at every tp after the anchor, up to and including
// the Read's tp.
//
// This test exercises a structural property of `A == {} since B == {}`
// with separate request/response tps: `A == {}` only holds at
// Heartbeat_response tps, so it's always false at Heartbeat_request
// tps and at the Read's own tp. Both traces below have at least one
// tp in the interval (Login, Read] where A doesn't hold, so the
// `since` never satisfies its continuity requirement. Neither trace
// fires. Confirming this is the test's purpose: to guard against the
// compiler erroneously firing `since` when A is false at some
// intermediate tp.
//
// trace.log: intermediate Login_request/response for bob at @20/@21
// — A is false there because those aren't alice's Heartbeat tps.
// trace_2.log: no bob tps, but A is still false at Heartbeat_request
// tps (@10, @20, @30) and at the Read's own tp (@32).
//
// A `since` on resolved forms like this is rarely useful in a real
// policy — request and response events don't align on every tp, so
// continuity is effectively unsatisfiable. A realistic pattern
// wraps A in `formerly within Δ`, e.g. `(formerly within 5s Heartbeat == {})
// since Login == {}`, which says "at every tp since the Login, there
// has been a recent heartbeat."

permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    Drupe::Action::"Heartbeat"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
    since within 1h Drupe::Action::"Login"::request{ input.user: context.input.user }
};
